kde3-qt: Qt 3.3.8 for kde3-revival
==================================

This is Qt 3.3.8b (qt-x11-free), maintained by the kde3-revival project
(https://github.com/kde3-revival) as the base for running KDE 3.5 on
current Linux and BSD systems.

It starts from the unmodified Trolltech release (the first commit in the
history) and only adds fixes: building with current compilers and
libraries, compiler warnings, and real bugs found on the way. It is
otherwise the Qt 3 API you know; the original change logs are in
changes-3.3.8 and changes-3.3.8b.


Differences from stock Qt 3.3.8
-------------------------------

* Library names. The libraries are called libkde3-qt.so.3 (threaded),
  libkde3-qt-st.so.3 (single-threaded) and libkde3-qui.so.1 instead of
  libqt-mt / libqt / libqui, the pkg-config file is kde3-qt.pc and the
  plugin build key starts with "kde3-revival". This Qt is not binary
  compatible with stock Qt 3.3.8 where fixing a bug required changing the
  ABI, so programs built for one must never load the other.

* Platforms. Only X11 on Linux, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD with g++ is
  supported (see PLATFORMS). Support for Windows, Mac OS, Qt/Embedded and
  the old commercial Unices has been removed.

* Compilers. Builds with current GCC in its default C++ standard (C++20
  for GCC 16); almost all compiler warnings have been fixed, the few
  remaining ones are being worked on. The headers need C++11 or later.

* System libraries. zlib, libpng (1.6), libjpeg and libmng are always
  used from the system; the bundled copies were removed. The SQLite 2
  driver (bundled sqlite) and NIS printer lookup were removed as well.

* Defaults. A plain ./configure builds the configuration kde3-revival
  uses: shared, release, threads, STL, GIF, tablet (XInput) and all the
  X11 extensions found on the system.

* GLib event loop (optional). "./configure -glib-eventloop" builds a
  QEventLoop that runs on the default GLib main context instead of
  select(), so that GLib based libraries (GIO/GDBus, libpulse's GLib
  main loop, ...) can be used in Qt programs. See "The GLib event loop"
  below. The default is still the select() loop.

* Fixes. Many bugs were fixed along the way (buffer overflows, use of
  uninitialized data, wrong operator precedence, missing breaks in
  switches, image and codec bugs, ...). Each fix is a separate commit
  with an explanation; see "git log".


Requirements
------------

* g++ (any current version) and GNU make
* X11 development files: libX11, libXext, libXrender, libXrandr,
  libXcursor, libXinerama, libXi, libXmu, libSM/libICE, libXft,
  fontconfig, freetype2
* zlib, libpng, libjpeg, libmng
* OpenGL (libGL, libGLU), optional
* CUPS, optional

bison and flex are only needed to regenerate the moc parser after
changing src/moc/moc.y or moc.l (see src/moc/README).


Building
--------

To build and use Qt in the source directory:

    ./configure
    make

To build it for installation where kde3-revival expects it:

    ./configure -prefix /opt/kde3-qt
    make
    make install

Run "./configure -help" for all options. The build needs no environment
variables; to build your own programs against an uninstalled tree, set
QTDIR to the source directory and put $QTDIR/bin first in PATH.

"git clean -Xdf" removes all build output (the equivalent of
"make distclean"; it deletes only files ignored by git).


Using it
--------

* qmake: put $QTDIR/bin (or /opt/kde3-qt/bin) first in PATH; projects
  with CONFIG += qt link libkde3-qt automatically.
* pkg-config: "pkg-config --cflags --libs kde3-qt" (add
  /opt/kde3-qt/lib/pkgconfig to PKG_CONFIG_PATH).
* Other build systems: link -lkde3-qt (instead of -lqt-mt) and define
  QT_THREAD_SUPPORT; include and library directories are
  /opt/kde3-qt/include and /opt/kde3-qt/lib.


The GLib event loop
-------------------

With "./configure -glib-eventloop" (needs GLib 2.36 or later) the event
loop is built on the default GMainContext (src/kernel/
qeventloop_x11_glib.cpp); qconfig.h then defines QT_GLIB_EVENTLOOP, so
programs and build systems can test for it:

    #include <qglobal.h>
    #if !defined(QT_GLIB_EVENTLOOP)
    #error this program needs kde3-qt configured with -glib-eventloop
    #endif

The thread that creates the event loop (the GUI thread) owns the
context. GLib sources (g_idle_add, g_timeout_add, GDBus, GTask
callbacks, ...) are dispatched by QApplication::exec(), and Qt's
timers, socket notifiers, X11 and posted events are dispatched by
g_main_loop_run() and g_main_context_iteration() on the default
context. Differences from the select() loop:

* processEvents() with ExcludeUserInput, ExcludeSocketNotifiers or the
  X11 ExcludeTimers flag handles only Qt's own events; GLib sources run
  at the next full iteration (so waits like the clipboard's are not
  re-entered by D-Bus callbacks).
* Zero timers (QTimer::start( 0 )) run at G_PRIORITY_DEFAULT_IDLE, so
  they do not starve g_idle_add() sources.
* Timers use the monotonic clock, socket notifiers have no FD_SETSIZE
  limit.

Rules and caveats:

* The thread that creates the event loop (normally the GUI thread, with
  the QApplication) acquires the default context for the whole lifetime
  of the loop. Other threads cannot iterate it: in a worker thread,
  g_main_context_iteration( NULL, FALSE ) returns FALSE at once, and
  g_main_context_iteration( NULL, TRUE ) or g_main_loop_run() on the
  default context block until the loop is destroyed. Worker threads
  need their own context (g_main_context_push_thread_default()). As
  with the select() loop, qApp->processEvents() must not be called from
  other threads; with the GLib loop it returns without doing anything,
  or hangs with WaitForMore. If another thread already owns the default
  context when the loop is created, the loop runs Qt's sources only and
  never dispatches GLib sources; this is decided once, at construction.
* processEvents() with ExcludeUserInput, ExcludeSocketNotifiers or
  ExcludeTimers does not run GLib sources. Never wait for a GDBus reply
  or another GLib result in a loop of such calls (as KMail, KAddressBook
  and kdm do for other things): it would wait forever. Use a normal
  nested loop (QEventLoop::enterLoop(), QDialog::exec()) instead.
* Zero timers yield to idle sources, but run from the normal timer
  source as well once they have waited for 20 ms, so a source that is
  always ready (a fast stream, a chain of posted events) cannot starve
  them. Such a source can still starve g_idle_add() sources, as in any
  GLib program.
* poll() reports a hang-up or an error even for an exception notifier
  (QSocketNotifier::Exception), which select() does not; such a notifier
  is not polled until the condition goes away, which the loop checks on
  every iteration and at least every 50 ms.

GLib callbacks run with qt_mutex held, as Qt's event handlers do.
Note that <gio/gio.h> (and other GObject headers) must be included
before any Qt header, because Qt 3 #defines "signals" and some GLib
structures have a member of that name.

The behaviour tests in tests/eventloop compare both loops.


